the gun fetishist right wing is in a  tizzy  after retired Supreme Court Justice John  Paul Stevens proposed that activists  currently activating for gun safety  should be pushing not for background  checks or for an increased age limit on  gun ownership but that they should be  pushing for a full repeal of the Second  Amendment and John Paul Stevens wrote  this in a New York Times op-ed yesterday  and after realizing many right-wingers  that the op-ed was written not by Led  Zeppelin bassist John Paul Jones but  rather by 97 year old retired Supreme  Court Justice John Paul Stevens they  went bonkers calling for his head and  his point was the demands that the  students who have been activating that  the protesters that who have been  activating recently that wave of demands  which includes good demands right  restricting access to some weapons  increasing the gun buying age to 21  background checks all that stuff he says  that that's all fine well and good but  he is taking the approach of encouraging  protesters to campaign for the outright  repeal of the Second Amendment to the  Constitution he believes that if we were  able to get rid of that second amendment  that we would be set up on sort of the  best and most effective path forward  when it comes to gun violence and not  surprisingly there's people on the left  and right that are frustrated with him  obviously the gun fetishist right is  straight up calling this treasonous but  there's even people on the Left who are  taking positions that either a second  amendment repeal goes too far like there  are many people on the Left who are gun  owners and believe that we have a Second  Amendment and regulation is okay  background checks etc but that the  Second Amendment should stay or there is  also an approach from the left which I  am more sympathetic to which is that  this is a very unproductive suggestion  from the retired Supreme Court justice  because it just won't work and I happen  to agree with that  I believe that focusing on a repeal of  the Second Amendment is a mistake in its  sheer impracticality you will get more  done by reforming the system that we  have and you will lose people and  probably create more enemies to your  cause if you jump to  let's repeal the Second Amendment and  I'm not coming at this from a  constitutional originalist point of view  if you've been watching the show  listening to this program for any length  of time you know that I believe that  there's no possible way that a document  written in the 18th century and then  amended a couple of dozen times years  and years ago could be fully relevant in  2018  I believe in continuing to make changes  and in sort of modern interpretations of  the Constitution so I'm not coming at it  from that point of view I hate that guns  are this much a part of our culture but  they are and with the number of guns  that are out there with the opposition  that you would create by making a repeal  of the Second Amendment your agenda I  think it's just a losing approach and  there's sort of two approaches to  achieving big change in politics big  picture right one approaches the Ted  Kennedy approach the incremental  approach which is any small step you can  take in the direction you want you  should take and over time a lot of small  steps will add up to bigger change  that's one approach the other approach  is you've got to start with the most not  extreme but the the the most radical  position the most changing position and  negotiate to the middle I don't think  that one strategy is right for all  issues health care for example I think  the left should start the conversation  at something far more progressive than  slight tweaks to Obamacare and this was  a criticism of President Obama when he  negotiated Obamacare which is he should  have started with something much more to  the left to end up negotiating to a more  progressive middle we can debate whether  that's an accurate criticism or not but  I be